      "author": "@e.feminella",
      "text": "Less code doesn’t equate to better code. For vibe coders that aren’t software engineers that might be ok, but otherwise I’d advise against it. You’ll end up with a hundred features that you build over time and 25-40% of it will be duplicated code. The using third party or system libraries part will help, but it’ll only get you so far.\n\nA much more reliable (and repeatable) approach is to clearly define architectural boundaries via rules (that load deterministically) these should include standard disciplines such as SOLID Principles, abstraction, single responsibility, separation of concerns, etc. And Claude will then leverage reuse instead of reinventing the wheel every turn.\n\nThis is significantly better in more ways than I have time to outline, but for those who have been written software for decades, it’ll intuitively make sense.",
      "likes": 0
    },
